# LLVM Patches

Patches applied to the vendored LLVM (`Lib/llvm-runtimes`) on top of the
upstream release tag. These are carried as a downstream fork branch
(`kairolang/llvm-project @ kairo-llvm-22.1.0`) and mirrored here as
standalone `.patch` files for review, re-application across version bumps,
and upstreaming.

## Current base

- Upstream tag: `llvmorg-22.1.0`
- Fork branch: `kairo-llvm-22.1.0` (upstream tag + patches below)
- Submodule `Lib/llvm-runtimes` is pinned to that branch.

## Patches

### 0001-file-tokenizer-hook.patch

Adds a `FileTokenizer` hook to the Clang preprocessor: an embedder may
supply pre-lexed tokens for a `FileID` instead of having the preprocessor
lex its buffer. Generalizes the existing dependency-directives token
mechanism.

Used by `kcc` to make `#include "file.k"` work: when the preprocessor
enters a `.k` file, the hook returns lowered Kairo tokens located natively
into that file's `FileID`. The `.k` include traverses the normal include
path (works inside `#if`, mid-file, anywhere) with full preprocessor
fidelity, and diagnostics attribute back to the `.k` source with column
precision, no `#line`, no source map, no proxy file.

- Files: `clang/include/clang/Lex/{Lexer,Preprocessor}.h`,
  `clang/lib/Lex/{Lexer,PPLexerChange,Preprocessor}.cpp`
- Size: 5 files, ~55 insertions, ~6 deletions
- Upstream status: RFC pending (see below)

### 0002-cxx23-compile-fixes.patch

Lets the tree build as C++23: a missing module header include in
`IncrementalParser.h`, `CXDiagnosticSetImpl`'s constructor moved into its
header, and `<cstdlib>` in `ProgramStack.cpp`.

- Files: `clang/lib/Interpreter/IncrementalParser.h`,
  `clang/tools/libclang/CIndexDiagnostic.{h,cpp}`, `llvm/lib/Support/ProgramStack.cpp`
- Upstream status: candidate (build fixes)

### 0003-float128.patch

`__float128` on every release target, squashed from the fork's four commits
(`c20043286c99`, `a489978e3dce`, `a4f9e83cfb4f`, `8f7e268535dd`); Kairo's `f128`
needs it:

- AArch64 (Darwin included), ARM, RISC-V: enable it with the right ABI handling,
  define `__SIZEOF_FLOAT128__`.
- X86: `HasFloat128` (IEEE binary128).
- MSVC targets: mangle it as an artificial struct in the `__clang` namespace.
- WebAssembly: `bf16`/`f16` in function signatures and the runtime libcall signatures.
- compiler-rt: the binary128 builtins (`CMakeLists.txt`, `trunctfbf2.c`).

- Files: `clang/lib/AST/MicrosoftMangle.cpp`,
  `clang/lib/Basic/Targets/{AArch64.cpp,ARM.cpp,RISCV.cpp,RISCV.h,WebAssembly.h,X86.h}`,
  `compiler-rt/lib/builtins/{CMakeLists.txt,trunctfbf2.c}`,
  `llvm/lib/Target/WebAssembly/{WebAssemblyISelLowering,WebAssemblyRuntimeLibcallSignatures}.cpp`

### 0004-int128-32bit.patch

`__int128` on 32-bit targets, fork commit `a26664f6051c` (2026-11-09); Kairo's `i128`
and `u128` need it everywhere:

- `hasInt128Type()` is true on i386 (Windows i686 included), ARM, RISC-V 32,
  PowerPC 32 (not AIX) and MIPS o32, with the layout `-fforce-enable-int128` gives
  (size 16, align 16); `__SIZEOF_INT128__` is defined.
- CodeGenModule's data-layout consistency check skips `__int128` on 32-bit targets,
  as it already did under the flag.
- compiler-rt: the ti builtins (`__floattidf`, `__divti3`, ...) are always built on
  those arches.
- ABI: libc++'s `file_time_type` becomes `duration<__int128>` on i686 and armv7, so
  a sysroot built by an unpatched compiler must be rebuilt (ksys keys its cache on
  the compiler, so its roots rebuild on their own).
- Files: `clang/lib/Basic/Targets/{ARM,Mips,PPC,RISCV,X86}.h`,
  `clang/lib/CodeGen/CodeGenModule.cpp`, `compiler-rt/lib/builtins/CMakeLists.txt`,
  new and updated clang tests.

### 0005-libc-str-to-float-int128.patch

Fork commit `a47beecca719` (2026-11-09), needed by 0004: with `__int128` on i386,
LLVM libc's `UInt128` is `unsigned __int128`, and the x87 `long double` mantissa
(`BigInt<96>`) converts to it only explicitly. libc++ includes this header, so it
failed to compile for i686.

- Files: `libc/src/__support/str_to_float.h` (one line)

One file per change, in order (2026-11-08): 0001 and 0002 are one fork commit each, 0003
squashes the four `__float128` commits. Applied in order onto `llvmorg-22.1.0` they reproduce the fork's HEAD
(`a47beecca719` since 2026-11-09; `8f7e268535dd` before 0004/0005) exactly. 0004 and 0005
are `git format-patch` exports of one fork commit each. 0001 is exported from `kairo/file-tokenizer-hook`
(`fcee6a01`), whose diff is identical to the fork commit `2091b018` ("code").

## Applying manually

The fork branch already includes these, so a normal recursive checkout of
the submodule needs no manual step. To apply onto a fresh upstream tree:

    git checkout -b <branch> llvmorg-22.1.0
    git am .patches/000*.patch        # all five, in order
    # or one: git apply .patches/0001-file-tokenizer-hook.patch

## Version bumps

When moving to a new LLVM release:

1. In the fork, branch off the new tag: `git checkout -b kairo-llvm-<ver> llvmorg-<ver>`
2. Cherry-pick / re-apply each patch; resolve conflicts from upstream drift.
3. Regenerate the `.patch` files: `git format-patch llvmorg-<ver> -o patches/`
4. Update the base/branch references in this README.
5. Re-pin `Lib/llvm-runtimes` and update `.gitmodules` `branch`.

## Upstreaming

`0001-file-tokenizer-hook.patch` is intended for upstream submission as a
generalization of the dependency-directives mechanism. RFC: <link once posted>.
If accepted upstream, this patch is dropped and the submodule returns to a
stock LLVM release.